# Expansion Type Errors #

# A livelit declares the type its uses have, Expansion, and its #
# expand must produce something of that type. What gets checked, and #
# where the error lands, depends on expand's kind. #

# Functional: expand is an ordinary function, Model -> Expansion. It #
# is checked where it is written, like any other function, so an #
# error is on expand's body in the definition, as an ordinary type #
# error, and no use is marked (the Functional slides, 1 to 3). #

# Macro (Sec. 3.2.5): expand returns CODE, a quotation, and a use #
# means that code applied to the use's splices (Fig. 5). The code #
# must be a function taking each splice, at the type the splice's #
# code has, to Expansion (premise 5). The splices' code is the #
# client's, in each use, so this is checked at each use, and the #
# error is on the use. It names what went wrong in terms the #
# definition states -- Expansion, the splices, the code expand #
# returned -- not the function type the check is phrased with, #
# which appears nowhere in the program (the Macro slides, 1 to 3): #

#   the code, applied to its splices, is not of type Expansion; #
#   a parameter cannot take its splice; #
#   the code is not a function of its splices at all. #

# An error inside the quotation itself (quote 1 + "one" end) is also #
# marked where it is written, and the use says the code has an error. #

"see the six slides that follow"
